```python 
def triples_sum_to_zero(l:list):  
    l = sorted(l) # sorting list to use two pointer technique easily and also make sure we get all possible triplets in one pass through array (O(n log n))

    for i in range(len(l)-2):  # loop till last but second-last element because after that, there will be no third number left. O(n) complexity  
        if i > 0 and l[i] == l[i - 1]: continue;      # skip same elements to avoid duplicate triplets (O(n))   

        start = i + 1     # starting index for next part of array after current element. O(log n) complexity  
        end   = len(l)-1       # ending pointer in sorted list 

        while start < end:      # two pointers technique to find three elements that sum up zero (O(n))   
            if l[i] + l[start] + l[end] == 0 : return True   # found a triplet. O(1) complexity 

            elif l[i]+l[start]+l[end] < 0: start += 1;      # sum is less than zero, so increment the starting pointer (O(log n))   

            else : end -= 1 ;       # decrement ending index to reduce search space. O(n) complexity  

    return False  # if no triplet found in list then it returns false by default ie., when all three numbers are greater than sum (O(log n))     
```       
This script will work for any input size and also works efficiently even with large lists. The time complexities of this solution is O((n log n) + c*n), where 'c' can be the constant factor depending on whether we have to return immediately or continue until end, in worst case scenario when all three numbers are greater than sum (O(log^2 N)).

